BT 1 0 0 1 0 60 Tm /F1 9 Tf 11.5 TL (No it doesnt. Logistic regression just gives every one of the 96 values its own weight, multiplies them, adds them all up) Tj T* -0.116738 Tw (with the bias \(z = w1x1 + w2x2 + ... + w96x96 + b\) and then puts z into the sigmoid to get a probability. It treats every time) Tj T* 0 Tw (step like a separate column in a table and it has no idea that step 10 is right next to step 11. If you shuffled the columns) Tj T* (the same way for every signal and trained again you would get the same results just with shuffled weights. The only) Tj T* ("time" thing it has is that each position has its own weight, so it can learn like "high values here means MI". But if the) Tj T* (heartbeat is moved a little bit left or right the values land on different weights and the model can get it wrong.) Tj T* ET
